# Step 2: Timing System Design and Implementation

> Date: 2026-03-29 | Prerequisite: Step 1 (commit `a6c9f43`)

---

## 1. Core Problem

The timing approach from Step 1 has fundamental flaws:

| Problem | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `time.monotonic()` for GPU | Measures wall time, not GPU compute time |
| `cuda.synchronize()` global sync | Synchronizes all CUDA streams after each stage, blocking concurrency |
| Timing logic scattered in `infer()` | Coupled with inference, not reusable or toggleable |
| Aggregation logic in server | Server should not know the key format of `stage_timing` |
| No statistical analysis / no file output | Only outputs mean to terminal, no p50/p95/p99, no post-hoc analysis |

---

## 2. Timing Strategy for Each Component

### 2.1 Three Inference Stages (GPU) -> CUDA Event

| Stage | Device |
|-------|--------|
| Stage 1: SigLIP + prefix embed | GPU default stream |
| Stage 2: Gemma 2B prefill | GPU default stream |
| Stage 3: 10x denoise_step | GPU default stream |

CUDA Event advantage: `end_event.synchronize()` only waits for that event without blocking subsequent kernels;
`start.elapsed_time(end)` returns precise GPU time (ms), excluding Python scheduling delays.

### 2.2 Future Cache Components (CPU/GPU Mixed)

| Component | Device | Timing Approach |
|-----------|--------|-----------------|
| Gate / Judge / FAISS CPU search | CPU | `time.perf_counter_ns` |
| KeyBuilder / VectorStore GPU search | GPU (`cache_stream`) | CUDA Event |
| GPU<->CPU transfer | CUDA `transfer_stream` | CUDA Event |

-> The timing system must support both CUDA Event and CPU perf_counter backends, specified at probe registration time.

### 2.3 Stream Isolation

CUDA Events for Stage 1/2/3 are recorded on the default stream; CP search runs on cache_stream; they do not interfere with each other.

---

## 3. Design Goals

| Goal | Requirement |
|------|-------------|
| Precise GPU timing | CUDA Event, not global synchronize |
| Toggleable | When `enabled=False`, `measure()` is a no-op with zero overhead |
| Pluggable | Each component registers probes in `__init__`; probe set grows/shrinks dynamically |
| Task-level aggregation | `on_task_begin()` / `on_task_end()`; print summary at task end |
| Memory-first | Ring buffer stores records; write to disk at task end |
| No intrusion into inference code | Interceptor calls `timer.measure()`, no changes to `pi0_pytorch.py` |

